 "note": "Home of the hedgehog ice cream", "kind": "Beach cafe", "text": "The little cafe in the National Trust car park at Chapel Porth. Its hedgehog is a cone of Cornish ice cream, covered in clotted cream and rolled in roasted hazelnuts.", "x": 169802.0, "y": 49449.7, "poly": [[[-281.82, -1052.37], [-281.7, -1057.66], [-272.09, -1057.6], [-272.09, -1055.37], [-272.21, -1050.07], [-277.02, -1048.59], [-281.82, -1050.13]], [[2119.75, -128.54], [2119.86, -133.35], [2130.45, -133.29], [2130.45, -131.05], [2130.39, -127.13], [2130.34, -126.24], [2119.75, -126.3]], [[272.09, 1072.73], [272.21, 1067.44], [277.02, 1067.47], [281.82, 1067.5], [281.82, 1069.73], [281.7, 1075.02], [276.9, 1076.51], [272.09, 1074.97]], [[-2130.45, 148.42], [-2130.34, 143.61], [-2119.75, 143.67], [-2119.75, 145.9], [-2119.81, 149.82], [-2119.86, 150.71], [-2130.45, 150.65]]]}, {"name": "St Agnes Beacon", "note": "192 m: the top of the map", "kind": "Hill · 192 m", "text": "The Beacon rises 192 metres above the sea and can be seen from most of west Cornwall. Fires were lit here to pass on warnings and, later, to mark celebrations. A Bronze Age burial mound stands on the top, the last of a row of three. Old stories say the giant Bolster could stand with one foot here and the other on Carn Brea, six miles off, and that the heaps of stones on the hill were carried up by his wife in her apron when he was angry with her. The hill is heathland of heather and gorse, in the care of the National Trust.", "more": "https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/cornwall/st-agnes-and-chapel-porth", "x": 171010.0, "y": 50215.9, "poly": [[[25.21, -197.0], [25.67, -198.35], [26.97, -199.1], [28.92, -199.82], [31.21, -199.77], [33.51, -199.41], [35.46, -198.63], [36.76, -197.28], [37.21, -196.15], [37.21, -193.55], [36.76, -192.36], [35.46, -191.59], [33.51, -190.9], [31.21, -190.88], [28.92, -191.17], [26.97, -191.57], [25.67, -192.91], [25.21, -194.4]], [[718.15, 181.21], [718.61, 180.0], [719.91, 179.27], [721.85,